  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in Arizona totaled $22.6B in FY2026 to date. Obligations fell 3.0% year over year while federal buyers made 14,419 awards at an average award size of $1.6M. The Department of Defense and the Department of Homeland Security are the two largest buyers, accounting for $15.4B and $6B in obligations respectively. The largest contractors are RAYTHEON COMPANY at $6.2B, TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP at $4.6B, and FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO at $4.5B. Construction, missile and space manufacturing, and direct health and medical insurance carried the largest industry shares, with Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $6B, Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $5.2B, and Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers at $4.6B. Recent fiscal years show an upward move into FY25 and FY26 from earlier years, with FY25 at $23.3B and FY26 at $22.6B. These figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial, fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal spending by agency in Arizona?",
        "body": "Spending is highly concentrated. The Department of Defense obligates $15.4B and the Department of Homeland Security obligates $6B, together making up the bulk of obligations in the state. Other agencies are much smaller by comparison, for example the Department of Veterans Affairs at $334M and the General Services Administration at $186M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which vendors and industries dominate Arizona contract obligations?",
        "body": "A few large vendors capture a big share of obligations: RAYTHEON COMPANY at $6.2B, TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP at $4.6B, and FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO at $4.5B. At the industry level, Commercial and Institutional Building Construction leads at $6B, followed by Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $5.2B and Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers at $4.6B."
      }
    ]
  },
